IMPORT prelude
-- EVERY-EACH-QUANTIFIER-SPEC §11.0.2: SAYING THE ROLL OUTRIGHT.
--
-- A party type is normally open. `Tenant HAS name IS A STRING` has one
-- constructor and infinitely many values, so "every tenant" is not something
-- the machine can count out: a quantified rule has to be told which list of
-- people it ranges over. That list is the ROLL.
--
-- `IN tenants` says it outright. The older spelling reads the roll out of an
-- `elem t tenants` condition inside `WHO` (§11.0), and still works — see
-- `run-roll.l4`, which is that form's witness. When a rule writes both, `IN`
-- is the roll and the `elem` condition goes on doing what any other condition
-- does: narrowing.
--
-- Three things narrow the group, in this order: the roll supplies it, the cast
-- word (`Tenant`) drops entries the constructor did not build, and the whole
-- `WHO` condition drops the rest.

DECLARE Actor IS ONE OF
    Landlord HAS name IS A STRING
    Tenant   HAS name IS A STRING

DECLARE Action IS ONE OF
    Sign    HAS signer IS AN Actor
    Receipt HAS issuer IS AN Actor, to IS AN Actor

theLandlord MEANS Landlord OF "Ms Ng"
alice       MEANS Tenant OF "Alice"
bob         MEANS Tenant OF "Bob"
carol       MEANS Tenant OF "Carol"
tenants     MEANS LIST alice, bob, carol

-- 1. The headline form: a roll and nothing else. All three sign, the barrier
--    is satisfied, and the HENCE fires.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`in alone` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `in alone` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1
  PARTY bob   DOES Sign bob   AT 2
  PARTY carol DOES Sign carol AT 3

-- 2. The roll is really the group: only Alice signs, so the barrier does not
--    fire and what comes back is the two outstanding obligations, their
--    deadlines counted down from the day that passed.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`in incomplete` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `in incomplete` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1

-- 3. A condition beside the roll. The roll is the three tenants; the condition
--    drops Carol, so two signatures are enough.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`in and who` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        WHO    NOT (t EQUALS carol)
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `in and who` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1
  PARTY bob   DOES Sign bob   AT 2

-- 4. The cast word narrows the roll. This roll holds a landlord as well, and
--    `Tenant` leaves him out: only Alice is bound, and only she signs.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`cast narrows the roll` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN (LIST alice, theLandlord)
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `cast narrows the roll` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1

-- 5. No cast word at all: every entry of the roll is bound, landlord included,
--    so this one needs both signatures.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`bare variable in` MEANS
    EVERY x IN (LIST alice, theLandlord)
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY x)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `bare variable in` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice       DOES Sign alice       AT 1
  PARTY theLandlord DOES Sign theLandlord AT 2

-- 6. Both spellings at once. `IN` is the roll — here the single-name list —
--    and the `elem` condition is read as an ordinary condition, which every
--    member of that one-name roll passes. If the condition were taken as the
--    roll instead, this would want three signatures; Alice's alone suffices.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`in wins over elem` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN (LIST alice)
        WHO    elem t tenants
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `in wins over elem` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1

-- 7. And the other way round: the roll is all three, the `elem` condition
--    narrows it to two, so two signatures are enough.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`elem still narrows` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        WHO    elem t (LIST alice, bob)
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `elem still narrows` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1
  PARTY bob   DOES Sign bob   AT 2

-- 8. The roll under a fork. Each tenant carries their own copy of the
--    continuation with the member bound, so a receipt is owed once per
--    signature. Only Alice is receipted here, so what comes back is TWO
--    outstanding receipts with DIFFERENT deadlines — 3 days left on Bob's,
--    who signed on day 2, and 4 on Carol's, who signed on day 3. Different
--    clocks is the visible proof that each member carried its own copy.

--
--    This case is written this way on purpose. With a bare `HENCE FULFILLED`
--    and a trace in which everybody acts, a fork and a barrier both answer
--    FULFILLED, so that shape shows nothing; it takes a continuation that
--    names the member to see the difference. And a barrier could not be
--    written here at all: `ONCE ALL HAVE` fires once, for the group, so a
--    continuation naming `t` is REFUSED when the rule is run (the same
--    refusal run-barrier.l4's `whose tenancy` witnesses).
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`fork in` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN tenants
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        UPON   EACH
        HENCE  (PARTY theLandlord
                    MUST   Receipt (EXACTLY theLandlord) (EXACTLY t)
                    WITHIN 5)
        LEST   BREACH BY t

#TRACE `fork in` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1
  PARTY bob   DOES Sign bob   AT 2
  PARTY carol DOES Sign carol AT 3
  PARTY theLandlord DOES Receipt theLandlord alice AT 4

-- 9. The roll may be computed. It is an expression like any other, evaluated
--    once at arming, and it is bracketed here only because an unbracketed
--    application would be hard for a reader to see the end of.
GIVEN xs IS A LIST OF Actor
GIVETH A LIST OF Actor
`without carol` MEANS
    filter (GIVEN p YIELD NOT (p EQUALS carol)) xs

GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`computed roll` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN (`without carol` tenants)
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `computed roll` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1
  PARTY bob   DOES Sign bob   AT 2

-- 10. An empty roll. Nobody is bound, so a barrier's "all of them have acted"
--     holds from the start and the HENCE fires at once.
GIVETH A DEONTIC Actor Action
`empty roll` MEANS
    EVERY Tenant t IN EMPTY
        MUST   Sign (EXACTLY t)
        WITHIN 14
        ONCE   ALL HAVE
        HENCE  FULFILLED
        LEST   BREACH

#TRACE `empty roll` AT 0 WITH
  PARTY alice DOES Sign alice AT 1

-- 11. How the machine reads the rule back. Written into the directive itself
--     rather than named first, so that the printers see it: the answer below
--     is the rule re-rendered from what was understood, and the same line is
--     what the natural-language rendering is built from. A quantified rule
--     that is never handed a time and a list of events stays unarmed, and an
--     unarmed rule's answer is itself.
#EVAL (EVERY Tenant t IN tenants WHO NOT (t EQUALS carol) MUST Sign (EXACTLY t) WITHIN 14)
